1 NAME Alarich Johan /Allers/
2 GIVN Alarich Johan
2 _MARNM Allers
2 NICK Alerich Johan
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 MAR 1847
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bergen
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 APR 1904
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bergen
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BURI
2 DATE 17 APR 1904
2 PLAC Krematoriet i Gøteborg, Sverige.
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Kjøbmand
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alarich Johan Allers was born in Bergen, Norway on 12 March 1847, the son of Johan Allers (1803–1888), a rope-maker who had emigrated from Geestendorf near Cuxhaven in northern Germany, and Berthe Gurine Hille. He 
2 CONC was baptised on 5 April 1847 at Mariakirken under the name Alrich Johan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His destiny was shaped before he could speak. Among the godparents at his baptism stood his uncle, merchant Carsten Allers — the founder of the trading firm that bore his name. Carsten had no sons, only two daughters, and appears 
2 CONC to have earmarked his brother Johan's boy as heir from the very beginning. When Carsten died on 22 March 1862, just ten days after Alarich's fifteenth birthday, the firm Carsten Allers passed to the teenager. He would run it for f
2 CONC orty-two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Alarich, the firm traded in colonial goods and salted herring from Allersgården on Nordnes, one of Bergen's oldest commercial properties. He took on a partner, merchant Bernt Martin Bratland, whose son was christened Carsten
2 CONC  Allers Bratland in honour of the firm's founder — the two partners lived next door to each other. When Bratland died in 1890, Alarich bought out his widow Hanna for 29,000 kroner and became sole owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beyond commerce, Alarich was a sailor. In the 1860s he was among the handful of men who kept recreational sailing alive in Bergen. In 1873 he was elected to the first board of Bergens Seilbaadforening, serving three consecutive te
2 CONC rms. He raced his own boat — 6.8 tonnes — and finished second in the club's regatta of 1 August 1875. In 1895, now a race judge, he watched from the officials' boat as his son Arthur won first prize sailing a vessel named Clara, a
2 CONC fter Arthur's mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1870 Alarich married Clara Marie Carstens (1851–1925), daughter of the Russian-German physician Friedrich Carl Carstens. They had twelve children, seven of whom died in infancy — a grief that left its mark on the family. Five s
2 CONC urvived to adulthood: Arthur (who later won Olympic gold in sailing at Antwerp 1920), Clara (married Bjarne Tresselt), Victor, Hilda, and Johan Alfred.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the turn of 1904, weakened by arteriosclerosis, Alarich stepped back from the firm. On 4 February he formally inducted Arthur as co-signatory. He died on the evening of Thursday 14 April 1904 at his home, Fredriksbergsgade 3, a
2 CONC ged 57. His wife Clara Marie placed the death notice in two Bergen newspapers the following morning, signing alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral service was held at home on Saturday 16 April at six in the evening. Afterwards, the coffin was carried to the steamship DS Nornan and sailed to Sweden: Alarich Allers was cremated at the crematorium in Gothenburg. Nor
2 CONC way would not open its first crematorium until 1908. The parish register of Nykirken notes simply: 16/4 sent to the Crematorium in Gothenburg. Not buried. Clara Marie would later become chairman of the Norwegian Theosophical Socie
2 CONC ty, whose founder Helena Blavatsky had herself been cremated in 1891.
2 CONT
2 CONT His estate was registered with Bergen probate court as case no. 2/604.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Mariakirken ministerialbok 1847 (birth and baptism); Bergens Adressecontoirs Efterretninger 16 Jan 1875 (firm registration); Bergens Annonce Tidende 9 Oct 1890 (property transfer); Norsk Kundgjørelsestidende 23 Feb 1904 (
2 CONC succession); Bergens Tidende and Bergens Aftenblad 15 Apr 1904 (death notices); Nykirken ministerialbok 1899–1917 (cremation); Bergen probate journal 1904 (no. 2/604); Bergens Seilforening i 50 år: 1872–1922.
