1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Clara Marie Carstens was born on 12 October 1850 in Halbstadt, near Berdiansk by the Sea of Azov (present-day Ukraine), where her father Friedrich Carl Carstens served as a physician in the Russian Imperial medical
2 CONC  service. Her mother, Doris Westphalen, died in Sarepta (a Moravian Brethren colony near present-day Volgograd) on 23 August 1857, when Clara was six years old. The following year, her father married Alida Frederikke Serck of Berg
2 CONC en, who raised Clara and her younger sister Hilda Sophie for the next 42 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family settled in Bergen, Norway around 1867–68. Through her stepmother's Bergen network, Clara met the merchant Alarich Johan Allers (1847–1904), co-owner of the trading firm Carsten Allers, and married him in Bergen. The fam
2 CONC ily home was Fredriksbergsgade 3, a property owned by Alarich, which remained the family's centre until 1906. Clara gave birth to twelve children, of whom four died in infancy or early childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara Marie was a prominent figure in Bergen's cultural life. She performed as a soloist at Harmonien — the Bergen Music Society, forerunner of today's Bergen Philharmonic Orchestra — on multiple occasions, and ran a professional 
2 CONC singing school from her home («Stemmedannelse», bel canto vocal training). She sat on the committee for the Music Society's charity bazaar in 1900, and was among the signatories of the public appeal to establish a music conservato
2 CONC ry in Bergen, alongside Edvard Grieg's brothers Alex and John Grieg, composer Johan Halvorsen, and members of the Mowinckel and Kielland families.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1904, she sold the family home and managed a small pension at Furruhoug, Fjøsanger (1906–c.1909), before withdrawing to Slettene on Askøy (1910). In 1912 she transferred her remaining properties to her
2 CONC  sons Arthur and Victor. In her later years she led the Norwegian national lodge of the Theosophical Society as formand (chair). She died on 7 September 1925 at Hotel & Pension Ottilie Hansen, Kong Oscarsgate 31, Bergen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Norwegian censuses 1891, 1910, 1922; Bergens Aftenblad nekrolog September 1925; Max P. Serck, Die Geschichte des Geschlechtes Serck (Bergen, 1934); Oslo Museum OB.FS0893 (family photograph 1889).
2 CONT
2 CONT Written by Carl Ludvig Hedberg 16/08/2026
